    "body": "That is strange. What PowerShell and PSReadLine version are you using?\r\n\r\nBecause `[Console]::OutputEncoding` should only take effect at the point something is written directly to the console via .NET API or native Win32. (PowerShell has its own output machinery that bypasses that, always using `WriteConsoleW`). PSReadLine is writing to the console and temporarily setting `[Console]::OutputEncoding` to be able to print Unicode.\r\n\r\nI was able to use `Set-PSReadLineOption -ContinuationPrompt` in my profile and have it print Unicode correctly at least in PowerShell 7.2.0 & PSReadLine 2.1.\r\n\r\nIt is unlikely that `Set-PSReadLineOption` does any interpretation of the string, it is more likely that it gets mangled on output to the console which happens later. It should really just store that string as a property. PSReadLine & PowerShell are open source so you can check their code directly to see how this works if you're curious enough.\r\n\r\nSee https://github.com/PowerShell/PSReadLine/issues/2866#issuecomment-960788773 for a similar issue when using `InvokrPrompt`. Although note that the actual output of the continuation prompt should happen after you return from the prompt function in this case.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
